{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Bury me with my fathers.</b> The language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 49:29), meaning \"with my fathers,\" and he did not explain \"to the cave\" — for it is not \"with.\" And it is possible that the language of this verse is elliptical: \"Bury me and carry me to my fathers, to the cave,\" for thus he said, \"and you shall carry me out of Egypt\" (Genesis 47:30), \"and his sons carried him\" (Genesis 50:13). And it is possible that the word אֶל serves in many instances to mean \"with\": \"bury me with my fathers,\" as in \"a woman to her sister you shall not take\" (Leviticus 18:18), meaning \"with her sister\"; \"to the cave\" — in the cave, as in \"and into the ark you shall put the testimony\" (Exodus 25:21), \"behold, he is hidden among the vessels\" (I Samuel 10:22), and likewise (Genesis 23:19) \"and afterward Abraham buried… etc., in the cave of.\" And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 49:29) said that the reason for \"Bury me with my fathers\" is that they should go with Joseph, and Jacob had no need for that, but rather he now commanded all of them that they should bury him in the cave, just as he had made Joseph swear, for he feared that perhaps Pharaoh would not give Joseph permission to leave the land, so that he would not be detained in his realm. And surely you can see that Joseph needed to ingratiate himself with the house of Pharaoh, that they should petition on his behalf before Pharaoh that he might go, and he — Pharaoh — answered, \"as you have sworn\" (Genesis 50:6), for because of the oath he would be reconciled to this."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:18.860610157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}